the Navy has not fully assessed how this new operational concept impacts the required size and type of the logistics fleet. Third, Congress must consider whether to mandate increased ship self-defense equipment. In the current environment of renewed great power competition, the TAO-205\'s current limited defensive capabilities (primarily torpedo countermeasures and space for future upgrades) may be insufficient. While the Navy plans to rely on escort vessels for protection, critics argue that equipping oilers with more robust active defense systems, such as Close In Weapon Systems (CIWS), is necessary to ensure survivability in contested waters, despite the associated cost increases.\n\nIn conclusion, the TAO-205 program is a vital component of the Navy\'s 355-ship fleet goal. However, successful execution requires congressional oversight on funding accuracy, a rigorous reassessment of logistics requirements under new operational doctrines, and a strategic decision on the level of self-defense necessary to protect these critical assets in a high-threat environment.', refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=None, audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[], reasoning=None), stop_reason=None, token_ids=None)], created=1783841042, model='Qwen3.5-27B', object='chat.completion', service_tier=None, system_fingerprint=None, usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=586, prompt_tokens=4937, total_tokens=5523, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), prompt_logprobs=None, prompt_token_ids=None, kv_transfer_params=None)
