rozen pay increases in the majority of years. Specifically, adjustments were accepted 13 times but denied 16 times between 1994 and 2019. These prohibitions are typically embedded in annual appropriations bills or continuing resolutions rather than standalone legislation. For instance, potential increases ranging from 0.9% to 2.6% were blocked annually from 2010 through 2019, often citing the need to match or freeze GS employee pay. Consequently, if Members had received every ECI-prescribed adjustment since 1992, the 2019 salary would have been approximately $210,900; instead, it remained at $174,000.\n\nThe report also highlights ongoing legislative efforts to alter the compensation structure. Numerous bills have been introduced in recent Congresses to repeal the automatic adjustment provision, link pay to specific legislative achievements (such as passing a budget resolution or avoiding a government shutdown), or reduce salaries entirely. Notably, the "No Budget, No Pay" concept has been proposed to hold salaries in escrow if budget resolutions are not met by a specific deadline. Additionally, the document notes that when adjusted for inflation, the real value of Member salaries decreased by 15% between 2009 and 2019. The report concludes by referencing related CRS products covering retirement benefits, health insurance, and detailed historical salary tables dating back to 1789.', refusal=None, role='assistant', annotations=None, audio=None, function_call=None, tool_calls=[], reasoning=None), stop_reason=None, token_ids=None)], created=1783841274, model='Qwen3.5-27B', object='chat.completion', service_tier=None, system_fingerprint=None, usage=CompletionUsage(completion_tokens=679, prompt_tokens=8601, total_tokens=9280, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), prompt_logprobs=None, prompt_token_ids=None, kv_transfer_params=None)

